WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.368 --> 00:00:02.602 align:start size:97% position:3%
- There are a lot of people
on the anti-woke side

00:00:02.602 --> 00:00:03.837
who don't believe
in free speech.

00:00:03.837 --> 00:00:05.772 align:start size:88% position:13%
They don't believe in
debating people's ideas.

00:00:05.772 --> 00:00:07.474 align:start size:81% position:19%
They believe in
destroying people.

00:00:07.474 --> 00:00:09.976 align:start size:88% position:13%
There are a lot of
people who are unwilling

00:00:09.976 --> 00:00:12.312
to have a discussion with
somebody they disagree,

00:00:12.312 --> 00:00:14.781 align:start size:94% position:6%
and they spend their
entire time owning people.

00:00:14.781 --> 00:00:17.317 align:start size:94% position:6%
Now, look, I go on the BBC
and make them look bad,

00:00:17.317 --> 00:00:19.285
and we'll put a clip
out, but I, that's not,

00:00:19.285 --> 00:00:21.788 align:start size:94% position:6%
that's not who I am and
it's not what I do, right?

00:00:21.788 --> 00:00:23.456
And I think generally,

00:00:24.691 --> 00:00:25.625
look, it's hard to be,

00:00:25.625 --> 00:00:26.993
and we talked about it earlier,

00:00:26.993 --> 00:00:28.895 align:start size:97% position:3%
it's hard to be anti
something and be constructive.

00:00:28.895 --> 00:00:31.564
(pensive music)

00:00:36.302 --> 00:00:39.139
- I'm Dave Rubin and we
are at the Locals Studio

00:00:39.139 --> 00:00:40.273
here in Miami.

00:00:40.273 --> 00:00:41.508
And joining me today is

00:00:41.508 --> 00:00:45.178
the co-host of the
Trigonometry podcast,

00:00:45.178 --> 00:00:48.515 align:start size:97% position:3%
and author of "An Immigrant's
Love Letter to the West",

00:00:48.515 --> 00:00:49.949
Konstantin Kisin.

00:00:49.949 --> 00:00:52.318
Konstantin, this is bizarre

00:00:52.318 --> 00:00:54.421
that we have never
met in person before.

00:00:54.421 --> 00:00:56.122
- And it is awesome
that we now have.

00:00:56.122 --> 00:00:57.624
- We have.

00:00:57.624 --> 00:00:58.591
What should we do now?

00:00:58.591 --> 00:00:59.859
- Let's talk.

00:00:59.859 --> 00:01:03.029 align:start size:88% position:13%
- (chuckles) Let's
talk about stuff. Okay.

00:01:03.029 --> 00:01:04.330 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think some people
sort of think

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:07.534
you are like the British
version of me in a way.

00:01:07.534 --> 00:01:09.169
So what's the deal?

00:01:09.169 --> 00:01:10.437
- Well, I have a sidekick.

00:01:10.437 --> 00:01:11.538
- Can you give me
a little history-

00:01:11.538 --> 00:01:12.472 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Listen-
- Of your situation.

00:01:12.472 --> 00:01:13.406
- I'll be honest with you, man.

00:01:13.406 --> 00:01:14.641
I'm gonna spill the beans.

00:01:14.641 --> 00:01:17.277 align:start size:84% position:16%
I used to watch your
show religiously,

00:01:17.277 --> 00:01:20.680
particularly 2017-2018.

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:22.482
- Those crazy, formative years.

00:01:22.482 --> 00:01:23.950
- Yeah.

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:26.386 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I thought what you
were doing was incredible.

00:01:26.386 --> 00:01:28.755
And you showed what was possible

00:01:28.755 --> 00:01:31.057
for somebody who is
independent minded,

00:01:31.057 --> 00:01:34.160
who wanted to come
in and, you know,

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:35.728
interview interesting people

00:01:35.728 --> 00:01:37.730 align:start size:94% position:6%
from a position of perhaps
not knowing very much.

00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:39.666
And so I said to
my buddy, Francis,

00:01:39.666 --> 00:01:40.967 align:start size:84% position:16%
why don't we do
something like this?

00:01:40.967 --> 00:01:44.070
It is genuinely, seeing
you and Joe, you know,

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:47.407
to have these conversations,
was an incredible influence.

00:01:47.407 --> 00:01:49.442
And so I'm delighted
to be here with you.

00:01:49.442 --> 00:01:50.677
- Well, I'm glad that you are.

00:01:50.677 --> 00:01:53.379 align:start size:88% position:13%
Was nobody really doing
that in the UK yet?

00:01:53.379 --> 00:01:55.148 align:start size:84% position:16%
The more independent
minded stuff?

00:01:55.148 --> 00:01:56.850
Obviously there's a
gajillion chat shows,

00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:59.819 align:start size:97% position:3%
and the UK has a long history
of long form interviews

00:01:59.819 --> 00:02:01.054
and that kind of thing.

00:02:01.054 --> 00:02:02.322 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Yeah. I can't
think off the top.

00:02:02.322 --> 00:02:03.690 align:start size:84% position:16%
There are people,
there are other people

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:05.725 align:start size:88% position:13%
who have slightly
different conversations.

00:02:05.725 --> 00:02:10.029
But I think that mix of
levity with seriousness,

00:02:10.029 --> 00:02:12.298
and that is something that,

00:02:12.298 --> 00:02:15.068 align:start position:0%
it's kind of interesting, 'cause
we're both former standups.

00:02:15.068 --> 00:02:16.236
- Yeah.

00:02:16.236 --> 00:02:17.437 align:start size:84% position:16%
- And Francis still
does stand up.

00:02:17.437 --> 00:02:18.972 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I think that
gives you something,

00:02:18.972 --> 00:02:22.442
because you've always got
a way to release tension,

00:02:22.442 --> 00:02:23.710
if you need to.

00:02:23.710 --> 00:02:25.378
And for us, it's
been very important

00:02:25.378 --> 00:02:27.881
because, you know,
we interviewed a
woman, Dr. Ella Hill,

00:02:27.881 --> 00:02:29.883
who's a survivor of one
of these grooming gangs.

00:02:29.883 --> 00:02:31.417 align:start size:84% position:16%
I don't know if you're
familiar with them?

00:02:31.417 --> 00:02:32.619
- Yeah.

00:02:32.619 --> 00:02:33.853 align:start size:84% position:16%
- And we interviewed
her for an hour.

00:02:33.853 --> 00:02:35.388
And this isn't really
anything to do with us,

00:02:35.388 --> 00:02:36.856
but nonetheless,

00:02:36.856 --> 00:02:40.827 align:start position:0%
it's an interview about a victim
of the most heinous crimes,

00:02:40.827 --> 00:02:42.061
which is funny.

00:02:42.061 --> 00:02:43.663
It's funny. Right?

00:02:43.663 --> 00:02:46.432
And that I think
is the magic sauce.

00:02:46.432 --> 00:02:48.301
- Was she ready to
be funny about it?

00:02:48.301 --> 00:02:49.536
- Yeah, it's all down to her.

00:02:49.536 --> 00:02:51.070
We were just able to
play along, that's all.

00:02:51.070 --> 00:02:52.338
- Yeah.

00:02:52.338 --> 00:02:53.439
So you're, you're out of
the standup game, huh?

00:02:53.439 --> 00:02:55.875
- Yeah. I hated the lifestyle.

00:02:55.875 --> 00:02:57.377
I enjoyed being on stage,

00:02:57.377 --> 00:02:59.879 align:start size:97% position:3%
but driving around for
hours and all of that, nah.

00:02:59.879 --> 00:03:01.915 align:start size:84% position:16%
- That's funny,
because for me, too,

00:03:01.915 --> 00:03:04.184 align:start size:84% position:16%
I loved, loved,
loved doing standup,

00:03:04.184 --> 00:03:06.085 align:start size:88% position:13%
and, you know, I do gigs
still here and there

00:03:06.085 --> 00:03:07.687
and book tours and
that kind of thing.

00:03:07.687 --> 00:03:09.589
It was never the being on stage

00:03:09.589 --> 00:03:11.658 align:start size:97% position:3%
or being at the comedy clubs,
it was everything else.

00:03:11.658 --> 00:03:13.526 align:start size:84% position:16%
It was the life that
it could lead to

00:03:13.526 --> 00:03:16.629 align:start size:94% position:6%
that I couldn't deal
with, which I think is why

00:03:16.629 --> 00:03:18.998
so many comics end up on
drugs or dead, basically.

00:03:18.998 --> 00:03:22.068
- It's a, it's, look, there
are harder lives, obviously,

00:03:22.068 --> 00:03:23.303 align:start size:72% position:28%
to than-
- No, no, no.

00:03:23.303 --> 00:03:24.804 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Standing in front
of people and talking.

00:03:24.804 --> 00:03:26.105
- Working in rice fields, maybe.

00:03:26.105 --> 00:03:28.374
- You know, getting
home at 2:00 AM,

00:03:28.374 --> 00:03:32.478 align:start size:97% position:3%
waking up at, you know, 10:00
the next day is just not,

00:03:32.478 --> 00:03:33.980
I can't do it.

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:37.350
And now you and I both are
fathers, so it's not for me.

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:39.919
When lockdown happened
in the UK it forced me

00:03:39.919 --> 00:03:41.454
to really reevaluate it.

00:03:41.454 --> 00:03:43.756
And my wife, we've been
married for 20 years.

00:03:43.756 --> 00:03:45.825 align:start size:88% position:13%
We have the most
beautiful relationship.

00:03:45.825 --> 00:03:48.494
But I was working so hard
because we were trying

00:03:48.494 --> 00:03:49.862 align:start size:84% position:16%
to get Trigonometry
off the ground,

00:03:49.862 --> 00:03:51.965 align:start size:84% position:16%
we were doing
standup, both of us.

00:03:51.965 --> 00:03:53.132 align:start size:81% position:19%
Francis was doing-
- Oh, wow.

00:03:53.132 --> 00:03:54.867
You married a comic too?

00:03:54.867 --> 00:03:57.303
- Well, no, my, I
married a comic?

00:03:57.303 --> 00:03:58.738 align:start size:84% position:16%
No, no, I'm talking
about Francis.

00:03:58.738 --> 00:03:59.706 align:start size:97% position:3%
Francis, my co-worker.
- I thought you said my wife.

00:03:59.706 --> 00:04:01.007
I thought you said my wife,

00:04:01.007 --> 00:04:02.141
we did standup, she
did standup too?

00:04:02.141 --> 00:04:03.509
- Yeah, sorry. I maybe misspoke.

00:04:03.509 --> 00:04:04.744
- Oh.

00:04:04.744 --> 00:04:06.713 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Maybe Francis is my
work wife at this point.

00:04:06.713 --> 00:04:09.249 align:start size:81% position:19%
No, well, what
was happening was,

00:04:09.249 --> 00:04:12.986
basically both Francis and I
were working extremely hard.

00:04:12.986 --> 00:04:14.454
And by the time lockdown came,

00:04:14.454 --> 00:04:17.657 align:start size:97% position:3%
even though my wife and I
have a beautiful relationship,

00:04:17.657 --> 00:04:19.859
it was kind of like
lockdown, sort of,

00:04:19.859 --> 00:04:21.661
I wouldn't say it
saved our marriage,

00:04:21.661 --> 00:04:24.264 align:start size:97% position:3%
but our son certainly wouldn't
have been born without it.

00:04:24.264 --> 00:04:25.732
Do you know what I mean?

00:04:25.732 --> 00:04:27.600 align:start size:97% position:3%
Because suddenly I was like,
oh, oh, I'm at home now,

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:30.103 align:start size:88% position:13%
and maybe I should
actually spend some time

00:04:30.103 --> 00:04:31.604 align:start size:84% position:16%
with this person
that I claim to love.

00:04:31.604 --> 00:04:33.006
- So either I misheard you

00:04:33.006 --> 00:04:34.707
or you referred to
him as your wife.

00:04:34.707 --> 00:04:37.977
In either case, one of us is
not looking very good here.

00:04:37.977 --> 00:04:39.279
- Well, it's probably me.

00:04:39.279 --> 00:04:41.247
- (chuckles) Probably
not, probably not.

00:04:41.247 --> 00:04:43.416 align:start size:88% position:13%
So for people that just,
that don't know you,

00:04:43.416 --> 00:04:45.485
don't know your work, can
you just talk a little bit

00:04:45.485 --> 00:04:48.688 align:start size:97% position:3%
about your sort of childhood
and into what got you

00:04:48.688 --> 00:04:50.857 align:start size:88% position:13%
into sort of the
cultural political thing

00:04:50.857 --> 00:04:52.058
that we're kind
of in the mix of,

00:04:52.058 --> 00:04:53.926 align:start size:84% position:16%
and then we'll hit
the issues of the day.

00:04:53.926 --> 00:04:55.428
- Well, the short version of is,

00:04:55.428 --> 00:04:58.197
I was born in the Soviet
Union in the late eighties

00:04:58.197 --> 00:05:03.136 align:start size:97% position:3%
into a family that I was
imbued from a very young age

00:05:04.003 --> 00:05:05.438
with stories of my ancestors,

00:05:05.438 --> 00:05:08.941 align:start position:0%
people who starved in the gulags
for saying the wrong thing

00:05:08.941 --> 00:05:11.444
or who were really
severely punished.

00:05:11.444 --> 00:05:14.580
My grandfather, the way
I ended up in the UK,

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:18.284
is my parents, they were very
wealthy for like three years.

00:05:18.284 --> 00:05:20.153
And in that time gap,

00:05:20.153 --> 00:05:21.621 align:start size:88% position:13%
they sent me to boarding
school in England

00:05:21.621 --> 00:05:22.855 align:start size:84% position:16%
because my
grandfather was there.

00:05:22.855 --> 00:05:24.857 align:start size:84% position:16%
And the reason my
grandfather was there,

00:05:24.857 --> 00:05:27.627
that it was that in the late
seventies, early eighties,

00:05:27.627 --> 00:05:30.763
he criticized the Soviet
invasion of Afghanistan.

00:05:30.763 --> 00:05:33.499 align:start size:94% position:6%
And he was immediately
ostracized by his friends,

00:05:33.499 --> 00:05:36.169 align:start size:94% position:6%
fired from work, his
wife was fired from work,

00:05:36.169 --> 00:05:38.204
his children, that's
my father and my aunt,

00:05:38.204 --> 00:05:39.872
were kicked out of university,

00:05:39.872 --> 00:05:42.942 align:start size:88% position:13%
for him making this
one comment in private.

00:05:42.942 --> 00:05:47.647
And so when it came to living
in the UK and doing standup,

00:05:47.647 --> 00:05:50.383 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I saw this climate
that was happening,

00:05:50.383 --> 00:05:53.186
where suddenly it was very,
very sudden, at least to me,

00:05:53.186 --> 00:05:55.388
because I wasn't paying
that much attention,

00:05:55.388 --> 00:05:57.924
suddenly words became
this all powerful thing

00:05:57.924 --> 00:06:01.561 align:start size:97% position:3%
that was to be treated with
the utmost seriousness,

00:06:01.561 --> 00:06:03.830
and jokes could not
be treated as jokes.

00:06:03.830 --> 00:06:05.898 align:start size:88% position:13%
If you made a joke that
people didn't like,

00:06:05.898 --> 00:06:07.767
they couldn't just say,
"Oh, that's not funny."

00:06:07.767 --> 00:06:09.068
Now you had to be punished.

00:06:09.068 --> 00:06:11.337 align:start size:81% position:19%
And in addition
to that, you know,

00:06:11.337 --> 00:06:13.473
the one thing that I really,

00:06:13.473 --> 00:06:17.610
as an immigrant to the UK,
I found very unpleasant was

00:06:18.911 --> 00:06:21.781
this idea that all
our social problems,

00:06:21.781 --> 00:06:24.717 align:start size:97% position:3%
whether that's, you know,
Brexit or Trump or whatever,

00:06:24.717 --> 00:06:27.086 align:start size:97% position:3%
can be explained by claiming
that people are racist.

00:06:27.086 --> 00:06:28.855 align:start size:94% position:6%
And that's when I came
across your show actually,

00:06:28.855 --> 00:06:31.858 align:start size:84% position:16%
because I was trying
to understand,

00:06:31.858 --> 00:06:34.093
because I'd lived in
England for all my,

00:06:34.093 --> 00:06:36.562
most of my teenage life
and all of my adult life,

00:06:36.562 --> 00:06:38.398 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I'm a dark-skinned
immigrant,

00:06:38.398 --> 00:06:41.234
and I experienced racism once
or twice, but it was never,

00:06:41.234 --> 00:06:44.637
I never felt like it
was a racist country.

00:06:44.637 --> 00:06:47.373 align:start size:88% position:13%
And so when a
referendum like Brexit,

00:06:47.373 --> 00:06:48.941
where I voted to
remain, by the way,

00:06:48.941 --> 00:06:51.244
equivalently for
Hillary in 2016.

00:06:51.244 --> 00:06:52.512
- Interesting.

00:06:52.512 --> 00:06:54.514
- And Francis did too
as well, we both did.

00:06:54.514 --> 00:06:58.117
When the explanation for that
that was being offered to us

00:06:58.117 --> 00:07:00.553
was that half the country
was, I was just like, no,

00:07:00.553 --> 00:07:02.955
this is BS, this isn't true.

00:07:02.955 --> 00:07:04.991
And that, I think,
is part of the reason

00:07:04.991 --> 00:07:06.826
that we really started
to look at these things,

00:07:06.826 --> 00:07:09.262
because we felt our
own industry change.

00:07:09.262 --> 00:07:12.165 align:start size:97% position:3%
And also we looked around
at society and we just went,

00:07:12.165 --> 00:07:13.466
"Something is off.

00:07:13.466 --> 00:07:15.868
We're being told
a bunch of lies."

00:07:15.868 --> 00:07:17.970
And quite nasty ones as well.

00:07:17.970 --> 00:07:20.339 align:start size:84% position:16%
You know, for
Francis particularly,

00:07:20.339 --> 00:07:25.244 align:start size:94% position:6%
you know, his father is
an evil straight white man

00:07:25.244 --> 00:07:28.147
who married a woman from
Venezuela in the eighties

00:07:28.147 --> 00:07:30.716
when that was not the
done thing in England.

00:07:30.716 --> 00:07:35.054
And he voted Brexit, and
he was suddenly demonized.

00:07:35.054 --> 00:07:38.391 align:start size:88% position:13%
And it just, that
picture of our societies

00:07:38.391 --> 00:07:40.860 align:start size:88% position:13%
is so inaccurate that
it was very frustrating.

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:42.795 align:start size:88% position:13%
And I think Trigonometry
was partly born

00:07:42.795 --> 00:07:43.796
out of that frustration.

00:07:43.796 --> 00:07:44.931
- Right.

00:07:44.931 --> 00:07:46.299 align:start size:88% position:13%
It's interesting
because I wasn't talking

00:07:46.299 --> 00:07:48.134
about Brexit that much at first,

00:07:48.134 --> 00:07:50.970
but then because of the
Trump situation here,

00:07:50.970 --> 00:07:53.272 align:start size:94% position:6%
I started having a couple
of people from the UK

00:07:53.272 --> 00:07:55.007 align:start size:94% position:6%
and some Americans that
were talking about Brexit.

00:07:55.007 --> 00:07:57.343 align:start position:0%
And then suddenly I had all
these people in the UK watching,

00:07:57.343 --> 00:07:59.779 align:start size:97% position:3%
and I was like, well, I didn't
even intend that to happen.

00:07:59.779 --> 00:08:01.247 align:start size:88% position:13%
But there was
definitely a connection

00:08:01.247 --> 00:08:03.082
between Brexit and the
Trump thing for sure.

00:08:03.082 --> 00:08:05.184
- There was, and
the cultural forces

00:08:05.184 --> 00:08:07.787 align:start size:97% position:3%
that swirl around those
issues are largely the same.

00:08:07.787 --> 00:08:09.889 align:start size:94% position:6%
Now, you guys do
politics very differently

00:08:09.889 --> 00:08:11.157
to the way that we do.

00:08:11.157 --> 00:08:13.793
But fundamentally,
the way I see it,

00:08:13.793 --> 00:08:16.729
it was a sort of, it was
a rebellion of the people.

00:08:16.729 --> 00:08:18.998 align:start size:88% position:13%
Whether it was channeled
in the right way

00:08:18.998 --> 00:08:21.000
is a different conversation.

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:23.269 align:start size:84% position:16%
But people certainly
in the UK felt

00:08:23.269 --> 00:08:26.372
that a lot of their concerns
were not being, not only heard,

00:08:26.372 --> 00:08:28.174
but they weren't
even being aired.

00:08:28.174 --> 00:08:31.077
And so that I think is
why those two issues

00:08:31.077 --> 00:08:32.311
are very similar.

00:08:32.311 --> 00:08:34.881
- And did that change
your politics, then,

00:08:34.881 --> 00:08:38.084
once you saw the
reaction to Brexit?

00:08:38.084 --> 00:08:41.187 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, I think, like if I was
to somewhat try to pin you,

00:08:41.187 --> 00:08:43.623 align:start size:94% position:6%
I guess I would say you're
a classical liberal.

